#: docs/user-guide/concepts/incomplete-designs.md:26
msgid "The states of a design"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/incomplete-designs.md:28
msgid "A whole design is **executable** when every relationship an output depends on is valid and every required output has exactly one driver. Below that, the status line at the bottom of the window tells you what is still open, as counts and short phrases: _2 not yet defined_, _outputs incomplete_, _not causal_, _reads across domains_."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/incomplete-designs.md:34
msgid "The Simulate page turns the same facts into sentences with links: _tilt needs a value before simulation can step._ — _dimByTilt has no definition._ — _level has no valid definition._ Each _Show_ link selects the object. Nothing here is a failure; it is the list of what remains. Below those, with a hollow dot, the notes that stop nothing but explain the trace: _dimByTilt is a rule nothing applies yet._, with the fix that writes the value applying it."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/incomplete-designs.md:41
msgid "Why BDL is built this way"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/incomplete-designs.md:43
msgid "Design is discovered in an order that does not match the order of dependency. You know the lamp will have a light before you know the formula; you know a relationship reads _Temperature_ before you know whether temperature is a quantity in kelvin or a coarse _cold / warm / hot_. A tool that refused to hold the design until every piece was decided would push you back to sketches. BDL holds the design, checks the parts that can be checked, and reports the rest as _open_ — with the same precision it uses for errors, so that \"what is left\" is always a concrete list."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/incomplete-designs.md:52
msgid "The practical consequences:"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/incomplete-designs.md:54
msgid "A relationship without inputs and without a formula is a **Source**, not a declared one: it is exactly what the simulator asks you for. A sensor reading is a Source with a domain. \"Comes from the environment\" is a complete state, on purpose; give the Source a formula and it becomes a computed value instead."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/incomplete-designs.md:58
msgid "An **open** relationship does not turn red when you change the concept it waits on; it is re-checked and becomes valid or invalid."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/incomplete-designs.md:60
msgid "An **undriven** required output is _incomplete_, an **undriven optional** output is fine, and a **contested** output (two drivers) is an error — because the first two are missing information and the third is a contradiction."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/incomplete-designs.md:64
msgid "Finishing a design"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/incomplete-designs.md:66
msgid "The Simulate page's list, the status line, and the **Fixes** section of an inspector all point at the same work. Fixes are actions the tool can offer for a finding — _Choose what Temperature is represented by_, _Connect a driver to light_, _Add a value that applies dimByTilt_ — each applied as an ordinary edit you can undo. A fix is ready when the tool can do it alone, a pop-up when you must choose, and a sentence saying why when it cannot be done yet; it never guesses."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/incomplete-designs.md:76
msgid "_For language implementers._ The kernel treats a declaration without a realization as a legal, unresolved declaration; the ladder a mapping climbs is _Declared → Open → Invalid → TypeValid → TemporallyValid → ClockConsistent_, and output completeness and hardware feasibility are properties of the design and of a (design, target) pair, not rungs. `docs/architecture/compiler-pipeline.md` (\"Driver and result\") and ADR-0015."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/incomplete-designs.md:85
msgid "[Relationships](relationships.md) · [Status meanings](../reference/status-meanings.md) · [Troubleshooting: incomplete design](../troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:1
msgid "Physical outputs"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:3
msgid "A **physical output** is where a value leaves the design for the world: a light, a motor, a display, a buzzer. It is not a concept and not a relationship. It **accepts** one concept, **updates in** one timing domain, and is **driven** by exactly one value of the design."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:8
msgid "Do not confuse it with a relationship's _output_ — the concept a relationship produces. `dimByTilt` produces _Brightness_; _light_ is a physical output that accepts _Brightness_. The first is a meaning inside the design; the second is the lamp."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:13
msgid "The rules, in designer terms"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:15
msgid "Rule"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:15
msgid "Why"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:17
msgid "An output accepts **one concept**."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:17
msgid "The lamp shows a brightness, not \"a number\". Connecting an _Opacity_ would be a mistake the tool can catch."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:18
msgid "An output has **one timing domain**, and its driver must update in that domain."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:18
msgid "The physical device refreshes at the domain's rhythm; a driver on another rhythm would leave the output reading a value from an unrelated instant."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:19
msgid "An output is driven by a **value** — a relationship that reads nothing."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:19
msgid "A rule is something you apply; only a value can be _the_ brightness at this tick. `brightness = dimByTilt(tilt)` drives; `dimByTilt` cannot. Studio offers the connection only to values."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:20
msgid "An output has **at most one driver**."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:20
msgid "Two relationships driving one light is a contradiction in the design. BDL names it and does not pick a winner — no priorities, no last-writer. Combine the two values in a relationship and connect that."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:21
msgid "A **required** output must be driven for the design to be executable; an optional one may stay undriven."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:21
msgid "A lamp without its light is unfinished; an optional status LED is not."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:23
msgid "The states of an output"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:25
#: docs/user-guide/reference/formula-language.md:68
#: docs/user-guide/reference/status-meanings.md:9
#: docs/user-guide/reference/status-meanings.md:29
#: docs/user-guide/reference/status-meanings.md:45
#: docs/user-guide/reference/status-meanings.md:56
#: docs/user-guide/reference/status-meanings.md:67
#: docs/user-guide/reference/status-meanings.md:75
#: docs/user-guide/reference/status-meanings.md:84
#: docs/user-guide/reference/terminology.md:8
#: docs/user-guide/studio/deploy.md:114
#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:116
msgid "Meaning"
msgstr "含义"

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:27
msgid "dashed sink, _no timing domain yet_"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:27
msgid "the output exists but has no domain; nothing can drive it yet and nothing is wrong"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:28
msgid "**undriven**"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:28
msgid "dashed sink; inspector: _Undriven — the design is incomplete without a driver._ (required) or _Undriven._ (optional)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:28
msgid "waiting for a driver"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:29
msgid "**driven**"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:29
msgid "solid sink; _Driven by brightness._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:29
msgid "complete"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:30
msgid "**ill-formed**"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:30
msgid "the sink shows the word _ill-formed_; the driver carries a finding: wrong concept, wrong domain, or a rule with inputs"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:30
msgid "the connection was recorded, and it is wrong"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:31
#: docs/user-guide/reference/terminology.md:36
msgid "**contested**"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:31
msgid "the sink shows the word _contested_; inspector lists every claimant"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:31
msgid "two or more drivers"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:33
msgid "Findings about a connection belong to the _connection_: the driving relationship itself stays valid, and the finding appears under that relationship's **Drives** section and under the output's **Driver** section."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:37
#: docs/user-guide/reference/status-meanings.md:7
msgid "On the canvas"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:39
msgid "An output is a **sink node** at the right edge of the canvas: a boundary bar and one input socket in the accepted concept's colour. Drag from a block's output socket onto the sink to connect; drag the link away from the sink and release on empty canvas to disconnect. Only a block of the accepted concept can land on a sink — a rule has no value to give, and a mapping block's output socket is its block's — and nothing reads from a sink."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:46
msgid "In the inspector"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:48
msgid "**Meaning** — name and description."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:49
msgid "**Output** — _Accepts_ (the concept), _Updates in_, _Required_."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:50
msgid "**Driver** — the state line, the claimants with _disconnect_ links, and a **Connect** pop-up listing the design's values of the accepted concept. When there is none, one sentence says so instead — _No value of Brightness in the design yet — a relationship that reads nothing and produces Brightness could drive this output._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:55
msgid "**Fixes** — for a contested output, _Detach … from …_ and _Create upstream combination mapping_; for an undriven one, _Connect a driver to …_ with a choice."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:59
msgid "On a relationship's own page, the **Drives** section offers an _Output_ pop-up to a value only. A rule shows the caption _A rule cannot drive an output — connect the value that applies this rule._ and, when exactly one value of the design applies it, names that value with a _Show_ link. A text-authored design may still write a rule as a driver; then the connection is recorded, the finding appears under _Drives_ and _Driver_, and _Disconnect_ removes it."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:66
msgid "From output to device"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:68
msgid "Which piece of hardware realises an output — a PWM channel, a digital output, an H-bridge — is not part of the design. It is a **device** binding, edited on the Deploy page, and the board it is placed on is a per-session choice. Changing either never changes the design's verdicts. See [Deploy](../studio/deploy.md)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:75
msgid "_For language implementers._ An output is an entry of `Ω` with an `OutputSpec { accepts, clock }`; a drive edge is `β d = o`. The well-formedness of a drive (`DriveWF`: exact type view and exact domain), the single-driver rule (`SingleDriver`) and completeness (`CompleteOutputs`) are checked by `bdl-output` after the semantic passes and never move a mapping down the status ladder (ADR-0015). `docs/guides/deployment-walkthrough.md` follows one value from a relationship to a pin. DI-20 records why only nullary relationships drive."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/physical-outputs.md:86
msgid "[Relationships](relationships.md) · [Timing](timing.md) · [Deploy](../studio/deploy.md) · [Troubleshooting: connections](../troubleshooting/connection-errors.md)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:1
#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:45
msgid "Relationships"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:3
msgid "A **relationship** says how one concept follows from others. `dimByTilt` reads _Tilt_ and produces _Brightness_; `adaptBrightness` reads _Brightness_ and _Ambient light_ and produces _Brightness_. A relationship has a name, a **signature** — the concepts it _reads_ and the one it _produces_ — and, once you write it, a **formula**."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:9
msgid "In the sidebar and the creation sheet, relationships are labelled _Mappings_. Same thing."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:12
msgid "Signature first, formula second"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:14
msgid "Creating a relationship only fixes its signature. That is enough for the rest of the design to be built around it: other relationships can apply it, an output can wait for it, the simulator can list what it still needs. The formula comes when you are ready, in the inspector's _Relationship_ section. Until then a relationship that reads something — a rule — is _declared_ (the inspector and the status line say so); one that reads nothing — a block on the canvas — is a **Source** — complete as it is, provided by the environment."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:22
msgid "The formula is checked against the signature as you type, and the check is in your own terms: _Brightness is a dimensionless quantity, but this formula produces an angle._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:26
msgid "Three shapes of relationship"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:28
msgid "Shape"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:28
msgid "Reads"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:28
#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:217
msgid "Formula"
msgstr "公式"

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:28
msgid "What it is"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:30
msgid "**Rule**"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:30
msgid "one or more concepts"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:30
#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:32
#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:168
#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:169
#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:170
#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:171
#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:173
#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:174
#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:175
msgid "yes"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:30
msgid "a function from concepts to a concept: `dimByTilt : Tilt → Brightness = Tilt / 90 deg`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:31
#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:32
msgid "nothing"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:31
msgid "none"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:31
msgid "a value the environment provides — from a sensor, a switch, an analog line. In simulation you type it; on a device the hardware supplies it"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:32
msgid "**Computed value**"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:32
msgid "a value of the design: `brightness = dimByTilt(tilt)`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:34
msgid "Only a relationship that _reads nothing_ is a value; a rule is something you apply. This matters in three places: a physical output can only be driven by a value; a formula applies a rule by calling it — `dimByTilt(tilt)` — and names a value by writing it — `tilt`; and only a value or a Source gives its concept a value at each tick. A rule _produces_ its concept in the sense of its signature — its output socket is that concept — but the concept is **carried** only by a value whose formula applies the rule. Until `brightness` exists, `dimByTilt` is a function nobody calls, and nothing carries _Brightness_."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:43
msgid "The creation sheet says which shape you are about to make as you change the reads: with nothing read, _Reads nothing: a Source…_; with something read, _Reads Tilt: a rule, a function to Brightness. It has no value of its own — a value's formula applies it…_. A computed value is a Source you then give a formula."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:49
msgid "Only a value shows in the simulator: a rule has no value per tick, so it has no column in the trace. A rule that nothing applies is a legal state, and the tool says so: the finding _dimByTilt is a rule nothing applies yet._ on the rule, in the inspector's _Relationship_ section and on the Simulate page, with the fix **Add a value that applies dimByTilt** — which creates `dimByTiltValue : () -> Brightness = dimByTilt(tilt)` when exactly one value produces each concept the rule reads, asks you to choose when several do, and says why when none does. On the canvas the rule's output socket is hollow — no value comes out of it yet — and, once the rule has a formula, its header reads _not applied_ instead of _rule_. It is not an error: nothing is wrong, something is still to be written."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:61
msgid "The formula"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:63
msgid "Formulas are short expressions over the names in scope: the concepts the relationship reads, the values and rules of the design, numbers with units, and a few keywords."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:74
msgid "A formula is checked for **dimension** (units must work out), for **meaning** (the result must be the concept the signature promises), and for **shape** (a rule cannot be used where a value is expected, and the other way round). The full language — operators, `if`, `let`, `match`, memory with `delay`, values from other domains with `sync` — is in [Formula language](../reference/formula-language.md); the editor is described in [Formula editor](../studio/formula-editor.md)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:82
msgid "Links on the canvas"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:84
msgid "Three kinds of link, all socket to socket in a concept's colour, told apart by where they end. From a block's output socket into a **mapping block**'s input socket: _this formula reads that block_ — `brightness`'s mapping block receives one from `tilt`. From a mapping block's output socket into the block beside it: _this formula defines that block_ — the one definition, drawn as a short joint. From a block's output socket into an output: _this block drives that output_. The read links are the **dependency**, read off the compiler's analysis of the formula, never off the text as you type it; they follow the formula. A rule (`dimByTilt`) is not on the canvas: the mapping block that applies it names it in its header, and the rule's own signature is edited in its inspector, in the sidebar. No link shows the arithmetic or an order of execution: the canvas is a picture of values and of dependency, not a flowchart."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:99
msgid "**Meaning** — name, description and _Role_: _Source_, _Rule_ or _Value_ (or the port the relationship backs), with one sentence."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:101
msgid "**Reads** / **Produces** — the signature, as chips and a pop-up with the concepts' glyphs. Changing either is an edit; the inspector says which relationships will be rechecked."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:104
msgid "**Relationship** — the formula editor: _Add definition_ / _Save definition_ / _Revert_ / _Detach definition_, the verdict line, and any findings about the formula; then, for a block, _Reads_ (the blocks the formula names — the canvas's links into it), _Applies_ (the rules it applies) and _Read by_ (the blocks whose formulas name it); for a rule, _Depends on_ and _Named in_ — as names you can click; then the findings about the relationship's place in the design — a rule nothing applies, with its fix beside it."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:111
msgid "**Timing** — _Updates in_: the timing domain, or _Any timing domain_ for a pure rule. See [Timing](timing.md)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:113
msgid "**Drives** — the physical output this value is the final target of, if any. See [Physical outputs](physical-outputs.md)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:115
msgid "**Fixes** — actions the tool offers for findings on this relationship."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:116
msgid "**Explain** — collapsed by default: the formal detail."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:120
msgid "_For language implementers._ A relationship is a declaration with an interface (the signature elaborated over the concepts' representations) and an optional realization (the formula elaborated to a Core term, a lambda over `rep` of each input ending in `mk` of the output). A declaration without a realization is the language's unresolved declaration, a first-class state. `docs/architecture/compiler-pipeline.md` passes 3–6; `docs/spec/textual-syntax.md` §11 for what elaborates; ADR-0013 for the formula language's naming rule. The links on the canvas are the kernel's `dependsOn` (`docs/spec/kernel.md` §5) — `refs` of the realization, carried per relationship in the analysis (`MappingAnalysis.references`, ADR-0034, ADR-0044)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:133
msgid "[Concepts](concepts.md) · [Incomplete designs](incomplete-designs.md) · [Formula editor](../studio/formula-editor.md) · [Formula language](../reference/formula-language.md) · [Simulate](../studio/simulate.md)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/timing.md:1
#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/timing-errors.md:1
msgid "Timing"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/timing.md:3
msgid "A product's values do not all move together. A touch sensor reports many times a second; a temperature sensor a few times a minute; a button when it is pressed. BDL makes those rhythms explicit, so that \"when does this update\" is a decision you can see, simulate and be warned about — not something that falls out of the firmware's loop order."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/timing.md:9
msgid "This page starts from the questions a designer asks and arrives at the three words the tool uses: **timing domain**, **delay**, **sync**."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/timing.md:12
msgid "How often does this sensor update?"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/timing.md:14
msgid "Every value that updates on its own — a sensor reading, a computed value that should refresh with it — belongs to a named **timing domain**. In the lamp, `tilt` and `brightness` both _update in_ **interaction**."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/timing.md:18
msgid "A domain is a **name**, not a rate. It says _these values tick together_; it does not say how often. How often is a property of the simulation (the period per domain on the Simulate page) or of the device, not of the design. Two domains with the same period are still two domains."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/timing.md:23
msgid "Create domains in the sidebar (**+** by _Timing domains_), assign them in a relationship's or output's inspector under **Timing › Updates in**. The domain's name is shown quietly at the right edge of the node."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/timing.md:27
msgid "When does this relationship run?"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/timing.md:29
msgid "A **rule** — a relationship with inputs, like `dimByTilt` — has no rhythm of its own. It is applied inside other formulas and runs whenever the value applying it updates. Its inspector says _Any timing domain_. Leave it there unless it reads a domain-bound value directly."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/timing.md:34
msgid "A **value** — a relationship that reads nothing — usually gets a domain: the sensor's, or the rhythm at which the product should refresh it. A value left on _Any timing domain_ is evaluated whenever any domain ticks (every tick, if the design has no domains at all), which is fine for a small design and imprecise for a real one. An output only accepts a driver that updates in the output's own domain, so a value that drives an output needs that domain."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/timing.md:41
msgid "What happens inside one tick"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/timing.md:43
msgid "Within one tick of a domain, every value in the domain is recomputed from the current inputs. There is no order to choose: the tool works out which value needs which, and evaluates in that order. Two values that need each other _in the same instant_ have no answer — that is an **instantaneous cycle**, reported as _These relationships depend on each other in the same instant: a, b._ The way out is memory."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/timing.md:50
msgid "Remembering last tick's value: `delay`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/timing.md:56
msgid "`delay(initial, expression)` is the value the expression had **last tick** — and `initial` before there was a last tick. This is how a design holds state: a running total, a previous reading to compare against, a latch. Because the previous value is explicit in the formula, a cycle through a `delay` is not instantaneous and is allowed."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/timing.md:62
msgid "`delay` may be written in a value's formula (a relationship without inputs), at the top level of the formula or inside a `let`, an `if` branch or a `match` scrutinee — not inside a rule that has inputs, and not inside a `match` arm or a block's result. The tool says so if you try."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/timing.md:67
msgid "Reading a value from another domain: `sync`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/timing.md:69
msgid "If `slowDisplay` (domain _display_) reads `brightness` (domain _interaction_) directly, the tool reports _reads across domains_. The two rhythms are unrelated, so \"the current brightness\" has no meaning in the display's tick."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/timing.md:77
msgid "`sync(domain, initial, expression)` reads the value the expression had at the source domain's **last activation strictly before** this tick, and `initial` before the source has ever activated. The initial value is required because the destination may tick first. The rule \"strictly before\" means a source value produced at the same global instant is not yet visible — you see it on the destination's next tick."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/timing.md:84
msgid "The same idea appears between component instances: connecting a provided port to a required port across domains asks you for a _Starts at_ value and records the transport on the binding ([Carrying a value across timing domains](../workflows/cross-domain-transport.md))."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/timing.md:89
msgid "Why BDL asks for an initial value"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/timing.md:91
msgid "Both `delay` and `sync` are places where a value is read before it has been produced — last tick at tick 0, the source domain before it has run. BDL does not invent a zero: you say what the product should show before the first real value exists, in the destination's own units."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/timing.md:96
msgid "What the simulator shows"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/timing.md:98
msgid "On the Simulate page each domain has a period; the _active_ column of the trace names the domains that ticked. A value whose domain did not activate keeps its cell empty for that tick. Memory and transports show up as values: a `delay` reads its initial value at tick 0, a `sync` reads the source's previous activation. See [Simulate](../studio/simulate.md)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/timing.md:106
msgid "_For language implementers._ Domains are the kernel's clock environment `Κ`; the per-declaration judgment is `Clocked`, and a direct cross-domain read fails it (`clock.cross_domain_reference`). `delay init e` and `sync src init e` are Core forms with explicit state cells; the tick is a two-phase read/write. `docs/spec/runtime-semantics.md`, `docs/architecture/compiler-pipeline.md` passes 8–10 and 14, DI-17 in `docs/archive/design-issues-ledger.md`. Frequency is deliberately not part of a domain's identity (`docs/spec/kernel.md`)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/timing.md:116
msgid "[Relationships](relationships.md) · [Simulate](../studio/simulate.md) · [Troubleshooting: timing](../troubleshooting/timing-errors.md) · [Formula language](../reference/formula-language.md)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:1
msgid "Your first behavior"
msgstr "你的第一个行为"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:3
msgid "**Goal.** A desk lamp whose brightness follows how far its head is tilted: upright is off, flat is full. By the end you will have a design with two concepts, two blocks of them, one rule, one timing domain and one physical output — checked, saved, and ready to [simulate](first-simulation.md)."
msgstr "**目标。** 一盏台灯，亮度随灯头倾斜的角度变化：竖直时熄灭，放平时最亮。完成后你会得到一个设计，包含两个概念、两个由它们生成的块、一条规则、一个时序域和一个物理输出——已检查、已保存，可以[仿真](first-simulation.md)。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:8
msgid "**Time.** About twenty minutes."
msgstr "**用时。** 大约二十分钟。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:10
msgid "You will meet each idea when the design needs it. The [Concepts](../concepts/concepts.md) pages explain each one properly afterwards."
msgstr "每个想法都会在设计需要它时出现。之后 [概念](../concepts/concepts.md) 页面会正式解释每一个。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:13
msgid "1. Create a project"
msgstr "1. 创建项目"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:15
msgid "Launch Studio ([Install and launch](install-and-launch.md))."
msgstr "启动 Studio（[安装与启动](install-and-launch.md)）。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:16
msgid "Click **New Project…**, choose a location and name the folder `lamp`."
msgstr "点击**新建项目…**，选择位置，把文件夹命名为 `lamp`。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:18
msgid "The workspace opens on the **Design** page: a sidebar on the left, an empty canvas in the middle, an inspector on the right, a status line and the page bar along the bottom."
msgstr "工作区在**设计**页打开：左侧是侧栏，中间是空白画布，右侧是检查器，底部是状态行和页面栏。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:22
msgid "2. Add the two concepts"
msgstr "2. 添加两个概念"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:24
msgid "A **concept** is a value with a meaning — and a template: the blocks on the canvas are made from it. The lamp has two: how far it is tilted, and how bright it is."
msgstr "**概念**是带有含义的值——也是一个模板：画布上的块由它生成。这盏灯有两个：倾斜了多少，以及有多亮。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:28
msgid "In the sidebar's **Project** tab, click **+** next to _Concepts_."
msgstr "在侧栏的**项目**标签页中，点击 _概念_ 旁的 **+**。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:29
msgid "In the _New concept_ sheet: Name `Tilt`. Under _Value_ choose **Quantity**, and for the unit choose **angle** (the symbol `rad` appears in its own column). Click **Create**."
msgstr "在 _新建概念_ 面板中：名称填 `Tilt`。在 _值_ 下选择**量**，单位选择**角度**（符号 `rad` 出现在它自己的一列）。点击**创建**。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:32
msgid "Again **+** next to _Concepts_: Name `Brightness`, **Quantity**, unit **no unit**. Click **Create**."
msgstr "再次点击 _概念_ 旁的 **+**：名称 `Brightness`，**量**，单位**无单位**。点击**创建**。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:35
msgid "The two concepts appear in the sidebar, not on the canvas: a concept is the template, a **block** of it is the thing that holds a value. The round shape in the sheet's preview means _quantity_; the colour is the concept's own and will mark every socket that carries _that_ concept. Brightness is a quantity with no unit: a level from 0 to 1."
msgstr "两个概念出现在侧栏里，而不是画布上：概念是模板，它的**块**才是持有值的东西。表单预览中的圆形表示 _物理量_；颜色是这个概念专属的，会标记每一个承载 _该_ 概念的插口。Brightness 是没有单位的物理量：0 到 1 之间的程度。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:42
msgid "A hollow ring instead of a filled socket would mean the value form is not chosen yet — that is allowed, and the guide comes back to it."
msgstr "如果插口不是实心而是空心圆环，表示值的形式尚未选择——这是允许的，指南后面会再谈到。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:44
msgid "**What you made.** Two named meanings. Nothing about numbers yet. The sidebar's **Library** tab offers ready-made concepts (_Tilt_ and _Brightness_ are both there)."
msgstr "**你做了什么。** 两个有名字的含义。还没有任何数字。侧栏的**库**标签页提供现成的概念（_Tilt_ 和 _Brightness_ 都在其中）。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:48
msgid "3. Put a block of each on the canvas"
msgstr "3. 在画布上各放一个块"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:50
msgid "A **block** is one value of a concept, updated once per tick. The lamp needs one tilt and one brightness."
msgstr "**块**是某个概念的一个值，每个 tick 更新一次。这盏灯需要一个倾斜和一个亮度。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:53
msgid "Right-click the empty canvas → **Add Block ▸** → **of Tilt**. A block named `tilt` lands where you clicked."
msgstr "右键点击空白画布 → **添加块 ▸** → **Tilt 的块**。名为 `tilt` 的块落在你点击的位置。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:55
msgid "Right-click again → **Add Block ▸** → **of Brightness**: a block `brightness`."
msgstr "再次右键 → **添加块 ▸** → **Brightness 的块**：得到块 `brightness`。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:58
msgid "Each block has one output socket on the right, in its concept's colour, and the word _Source_ in its header with a bar down its left edge: a block with no formula is **provided from outside** — the environment, a sensor — and stays so until you say how it is computed. That is not an error. You could stop here, save, and come back tomorrow."
msgstr "每个块右侧有一个输出插口，颜色是其概念的颜色；标题带有 _来源_ 一词，左边缘有一条竖线：没有公式的块由**外部提供**——环境、传感器——直到你说明它如何计算为止。这不是错误。你可以在这里停下，保存，明天再来。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:66
msgid "_Two blocks before the formula: tilt, and brightness still provided from outside._"
msgstr "_公式写出之前的两个块：tilt，以及仍由外部提供的 brightness。_"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:69
msgid "(Right-clicking the canvas and choosing **Add Block ▸ New Concept ▸** makes a concept _and_ a block of it in one go, where you clicked; dragging a Library row onto the canvas does the same.)"
msgstr "（右键点击画布并选择**添加块 ▸ 新概念 ▸**，会在你点击的位置一次创建一个概念 _以及_ 它的一个块；把库中的一行拖到画布上效果相同。）"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:73
msgid "4. Write the rule"
msgstr "4. 写出规则"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:75
msgid "How does brightness follow from the tilt? That is a **rule**: a relationship that reads a concept and produces another. A rule is a template too — it is applied inside a block's formula, and is not itself a block on the canvas."
msgstr "亮度如何由倾斜得出？这是一条**规则**：读取一个概念并生成另一个概念的关系。规则也是模板——它在块的公式里被应用，本身并不是画布上的块。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:79
msgid "Click **+** next to _Mappings_ in the sidebar (the sheet is titled _New mapping_; the guide says _relationship_ — they are the same thing)."
msgstr "点击侧栏中 _映射_ 旁的 **+**（面板标题是 _新建映射_；指南中说 _关系_——它们是同一回事）。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:81
msgid "Name `dimByTilt`. Under _Reads_, switch on **Tilt**. Under _Produces_, choose **Brightness**. Click **Create**."
msgstr "名称 `dimByTilt`。在 _读取_ 下打开 **Tilt**。在 _生成_ 下选择 **Brightness**。点击**创建**。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:84
msgid "`dimByTilt` appears in the sidebar's _Mappings_ list, selected, and the inspector shows it: _Rule_, reads Tilt, produces Brightness, no formula yet."
msgstr "`dimByTilt` 出现在侧栏的 _映射_ 列表中并被选中，检查器显示它：_规则_，读取 Tilt，生成 Brightness，尚无公式。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:87
msgid "In the **Relationship** section, the editor opens in its **Formula** view: an empty slot, `?`, and the words _produces a Brightness_. Click the slot. Under it, _References_ lists **Tilt** — click it. The slot becomes `Tilt`."
msgstr "在**关系**区，编辑器以**公式**视图打开：一个空槽位 `?`，以及文字 _生成 Brightness_。点击槽位。它下方的 _引用_ 列出了 **Tilt**——点击它。槽位变为 `Tilt`。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:90
msgid "Click `Tilt` and press **÷**. The formula reads `Tilt ÷ ?`, and the new slot is selected: _Expected: an angle, because an angle ÷ an angle = a dimensionless quantity._"
msgstr "点击 `Tilt` 并按 **÷**。公式显示为 `Tilt ÷ ?`，新槽位被选中：_期望：角度，因为角度 ÷ 角度 = 无量纲量。_"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:93
msgid "In the number entry type `90`, choose **deg** from the unit pop-up (only angle units are offered), and press Return. The formula reads `Tilt ÷ 90 deg` and the line under the field says _Valid definition_."
msgstr "在数字输入框中输入 `90`，从单位弹出菜单中选择 **deg**（只提供角度单位），按回车。公式显示为 `Tilt ÷ 90 deg`，字段下方一行显示 _有效的定义_。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:97
msgid "If you would rather type, switch to **Text** and write it as text:"
msgstr "如果你更愿意打字，切换到**文本**并以文本形式写："

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:103
msgid "Both views edit the same formula. Press **⌘↩** or click **Add definition**."
msgstr "两个视图编辑的是同一个公式。按 **⌘↩** 或点击**添加定义**。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:107
#: docs/user-guide/studio/formula-editor.md:57
msgid "_The Formula view with the denominator slot selected: the quotient drawn as a fraction; the compiler says the slot expects an angle and why, and offers a number with the angle units, the references that fit and the equations whose result fits._"
msgstr "_选中分母槽位的公式视图：商画成分数；编译器说明该槽位需要一个角度以及原因，并提供带角度单位的数字、匹配的引用以及结果匹配的方程。_"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:112
msgid "**What you made.** A rule: a brightness is a tilt divided by ninety degrees. Try it in the **Text** view: change `90 deg` to `90 s` and watch the line under the field turn red: it says what Brightness is and what the formula produces instead — an angle divided by a time is not a plain number. Put `deg` back. Every formula is checked this way, for units and for meaning, as you type or as you assemble it — and nothing is saved to the design until you press _Add definition_."
msgstr "**你做了什么。** 一条规则：亮度等于倾斜除以九十度。在**文本**视图里试试：把 `90 deg` 改成 `90 s`，看字段下方那一行变红：它说明 Brightness 是什么、而公式实际生成的又是什么——角度除以时间不是一个普通数字。把 `deg` 改回来。每个公式都会这样检查单位和含义，无论是键入还是拼装——而在你按下 _添加定义_ 之前，什么都不会保存到设计中。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:122
#: docs/user-guide/studio/formula-editor.md:160
msgid "_The formula field with a draft that does not check: the red verdict line says what Brightness is and what the formula produces instead._"
msgstr "_公式字段中有一个未通过检查的草稿：红色结论行说明 Brightness 是什么，以及公式实际生成的是什么。_"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:127
msgid "In the Text view, **⌃Space** opens completion: the names you can use here (_Tilt_), units after a number, keywords. Hovering a name for a moment shows what it is. The Formula view asks for nothing: each slot lists what fits."
msgstr "在文本视图中，**⌃Space** 打开补全：此处可用的名字（_Tilt_）、数字后的单位、关键字。把鼠标在名字上停留片刻会显示它是什么。公式视图什么都不需要你记：每个槽位都列出适合的内容。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:129
msgid "5. The tilt comes in from the environment"
msgstr "5. 倾斜来自环境"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:131
msgid "Where does the tilt come from? From the environment — a sensor, outside the design. In BDL a value the environment provides is a **Source**: a block with **no formula**. `tilt` already is one: it says _Source_ in its header, with the bar down its left edge and no input socket. Nothing is missing. In the simulator you will type its value; on a device the sensor will provide it."
msgstr "倾斜从哪里来？来自环境——设计之外的传感器。在 BDL 中，环境提供的值是**来源**：**没有公式**的块。`tilt` 已经是一个来源：标题写着 _来源_，左边缘有竖线，没有输入插口。什么都不缺。在仿真器中你将键入它的值；在设备上由传感器提供。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:137
msgid "6. Compute the lamp's brightness"
msgstr "6. 计算灯的亮度"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:139
msgid "The value the lamp actually shows is the rule applied to the tilt. That is `brightness`'s formula."
msgstr "灯实际显示的值，是把规则应用到倾斜上得到的。这就是 `brightness` 的公式。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:142
msgid "Click the `brightness` block. The inspector shows it."
msgstr "点击 `brightness` 块。检查器显示它。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:143
msgid "Enter the formula `dimByTilt(tilt)` — in the Formula view, click the slot and choose **dimByTilt** under _References_ (it becomes `dimByTilt(?)`), then click the new slot and choose **tilt**; or type it in the Text view. _Add definition._"
msgstr "输入公式 `dimByTilt(tilt)`——在公式视图中点击槽位，在 _引用_ 下选择 **dimByTilt**（它变成 `dimByTilt(?)`），再点击新槽位并选择 **tilt**；或者在文本视图中键入。_添加定义。_"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:148
msgid "Completion offers `dimByTilt(` because it is a rule, and `tilt` because it is a block. On the canvas a **mapping block** appears beside `brightness` — the formula, drawn as a node: its header names the rule it applies (`dimByTilt`), it has an input socket labelled `tilt` — one for each block the formula reads — and a link runs from `tilt`'s output socket into it; a short link joins it to `brightness`, the block it defines. `brightness`'s header no longer says _Source_: the value is computed. The rule's name is in the formula, where it is applied; the rule stays in the sidebar."
msgstr "补全会提供 `dimByTilt(`，因为它是规则；也提供 `tilt`，因为它是块。画布上，`brightness` 旁出现一个**映射块**——公式画成的节点：标题写着它应用的规则（`dimByTilt`），左侧有一个标着 `tilt` 的输入插槽——公式读取的每个块各一个——一条连线从 `tilt` 的输出插槽接入；一条短连线把它接到它所定义的块 `brightness`。`brightness` 的标题不再写 _Source_：这个值是算出来的。规则名在公式里，在应用它的地方；规则本身留在侧栏。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:160
msgid "You can also wire by dragging: drop `tilt`'s output socket on a block that has no formula yet, or on a hollow `?` socket of a mapping block with an open position, and the formula gets the name. What the canvas draws is what the formula says — the formula is the one place where blocks are combined."
msgstr "也可以拖拽连线：把 `tilt` 的输出插槽放到一个还没有公式的块上，或放到某个映射块中一个空缺位置的空心 `?` 插槽上，公式就得到这个名字。画布画的正是公式所说的——公式是把块组合起来的唯一地方。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:162
msgid "**What you made.** Two blocks — a Source (`tilt`) and a computed value (`brightness`) — and a rule (`dimByTilt`) the value applies. The status line at the bottom counts three relationships and one source."
msgstr "**你做了什么。** 两个块——一个来源（`tilt`）和一个计算值（`brightness`）——以及该值所应用的一条规则（`dimByTilt`）。底部的状态行计为三个关系和一个来源。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:166
msgid "7. Give the values a rhythm"
msgstr "7. 给值一个节奏"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:168
msgid "A value that comes from outside has a rhythm: the sensor reports every so often. In BDL that rhythm is a named **timing domain**, and every value that updates on its own belongs to one."
msgstr "来自外部的值有节奏：传感器每隔一段时间报告一次。在 BDL 中，这个节奏是一个有名字的**时序域**，每个自行更新的值都属于某一个。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:172
msgid "**+** next to _Timing domains_: name it `interaction`. Create."
msgstr "点击 _时序域_ 旁的 **+**：命名为 `interaction`。创建。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:173
msgid "Select `tilt`. In the inspector's **Timing** section, set **Updates in** to _interaction_."
msgstr "选中 `tilt`。在检查器的**时序**区，把**更新于**设为 _interaction_。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:175
msgid "Select `brightness` and do the same."
msgstr "选中 `brightness`，做同样的事。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:177
msgid "`dimByTilt` stays on _Any timing domain_: it is a pure rule and takes the rhythm of whatever applies it. The domain's name appears quietly at the right edge of the two blocks."
msgstr "`dimByTilt` 保持在 _任意时序域_：它是纯规则，跟随应用它的那个块的节奏。域名会安静地出现在两个块的右边缘。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:181
msgid "**What you made.** A design in which \"when does this update\" is an explicit decision. A domain is a name, not a rate — how often _interaction_ ticks is chosen when you simulate or deploy, not here."
msgstr "**你做了什么。** 一个把“这个什么时候更新”作为显式决定的设计。域是名字，不是速率——_interaction_ 多久走一拍是在仿真或部署时选择的，不在这里。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:185
msgid "8. Add the light"
msgstr "8. 添加灯"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:187
msgid "Brightness is a value inside the design. The lamp itself is a **physical output**: the place where a value leaves the design for the world."
msgstr "Brightness 是设计内部的值。灯本身是一个**物理输出**：值离开设计、进入世界的地方。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:190
msgid "**+** next to _Outputs_. In the _New output_ sheet: Name `light`, **Accepts** Brightness, **Updates in** interaction, **Required** on. Create."
msgstr "点击 _输出_ 旁的 **+**。在 _新建输出_ 面板中：名称 `light`，**接受** Brightness，**更新于** interaction，打开**必需**。创建。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:193
msgid "A sink node appears at the right edge of the canvas, drawn dashed: it has a domain but nothing drives it yet. The status line says _outputs incomplete_."
msgstr "画布右边缘出现一个汇节点，以虚线绘制：它有域，但还没有东西驱动它。状态行显示 _输出未完成_。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:196
msgid "Drag from `brightness`'s output socket onto the sink's socket. (Or select the output and pick `brightness` in the **Connect** pop-up of its inspector; the pop-up marks rules — relationships that _have inputs_ — which cannot drive an output.)"
msgstr "从 `brightness` 的输出插口拖到接收端的插口上。（或者选中输出，在其检查器的**连接**弹出菜单中选择 `brightness`；弹出菜单会标出规则——_有输入_ 的关系——它们不能驱动输出。）"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:201
msgid "The sink becomes solid. Only a block that produces the accepted concept in the same domain can drive an output: `brightness` qualifies, `tilt` does not (the wrong concept, and the socket refuses it), and a rule has no value to give."
msgstr "接收端变为实线。只有在同一时序域中生成所接受概念的块才能驱动输出：`brightness` 符合，`tilt` 不符合（概念不对，插口会拒绝），而规则没有值可给。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:205
msgid "**What you made.** A complete design. The status line no longer says _outputs incomplete_, and nothing is _not yet defined_: `tilt`, the Source, is meant to stay without a formula, and the line counts it as _1 source_. Press **⌘S** to save."
msgstr "**你做了什么。** 一个完整的设计。状态行不再显示 _输出未完成_，也没有任何 _尚未定义_ 的项：来源 `tilt` 本来就应该没有公式，状态行把它计为 _1 个来源_。按 **⌘S** 保存。"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:210
msgid "What you have"
msgstr "你现在拥有的"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:214
msgid "_The finished lamp: the Source tilt, the mapping block applying the rule dimByTilt, the block brightness it defines, and the driven light._"
msgstr "_完成的灯：来源 tilt、应用规则 dimByTilt 的映射块、它定义的块 brightness，以及被驱动的 light。_"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:217
msgid "Object"
msgstr "对象"

#: docs/user-guide/getting-started/first-behavior.md:217
msgid "Kind"
msgstr "种类"

